  "reply": "Yes, the Court allowed the government to use the proposed exhibits during the opening statement.\n\nThe Court granted the government's Motion to Use Photographs in Opening Statement (Doc. 122), finding that \"the government may use the proposed exhibits in its opening statement.\" Specifically:\n\n- For Exhibits 3B, 4D, 7A, 7E, 11A, 11B, and 21D, the defense did not object to admissibility, so the government could fairly expect them to come into evidence.\n- For Exhibits 9A, 11D, 11G, 11F, 14G, and the cropped version of 11I, the Court had already found them relevant and admissible.\n- For Exhibits 6F and 11C, the Court reserved ruling pending foundation, but found it would not be unfairly prejudicial or misleading for the government to use them in opening statement.\n\nThe Court did impose one restriction: the government could not refer to the plastic wrappers depicted in Exhibit 14G as \"kilo wrappers.\"",
